    {
      "calls": [
        {
          "ID": "toolu_01EyTRDgLMMp8hvVpG8q3bB1",
          "Name": "submit_read",
          "Input": {
            "assessments": [
              {
                "facts": [
                  "S1:F2",
                  "S1:F7",
                  "S2:F6",
                  "S6:F21",
                  "S6:F22",
                  "S7:F29",
                  "F26",
                  "F30"
                ],
                "ref": "N1",
                "statement": "London is not treating the US–Russia agreement and Trump's energy ceasefire as a reason to ease pressure on Moscow: within days it widened oil sanctions, stepped up air-defence and missile supply and ordered further packages, which reads as a hedge against the ceasefire failing.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "facts": [
                  "S8:F31",
                  "S8:F34",
                  "S9:F35",
                  "S11:F37"
                ],
                "ref": "N2",
                "statement": "This week's rail announcements put the public-ownership project's headquarters, its new apprentice intake and the new northern fleet's manufacturing all in Derby, making the city its industrial and administrative centre.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "facts": [
                  "S13:F51",
                  "S14:F56",
                  "F55",
                  "F57"
                ],
                "ref": "N3",
                "statement": "The Prime Minister's offer to Holyrood already comes with a concrete deal behind it, since the Scottish government matched the UK's £10 million for defence colleges, which suggests his promised cooperative style with the devolved government is starting to produce agreements.",
                "supersedes": ""
              }
            ],
            "statements": [
              {
                "cites": [
                  "L1",
                  "F49",
                  "F55"
                ],
                "text": "For background: Andy Burnham has been Prime Minister since 20 July 2026. When he arrived in Downing Street he said he wanted to do politics differently, and this was his first autumn of statements, visits and announcements."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S1:F2",
                  "S1:F7"
                ],
                "text": "The diplomacy on Ukraine was set by Washington. The US and Russia reached an agreement on 9 October, and two days later President Trump announced a reciprocal energy ceasefire."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S2:F6",
                  "S6:F22",
                  "S7:F29",
                  "S6:F21"
                ],
                "text": "Britain went the other way. The day after the ceasefire announcement, it said UK sanctions now cover over 90% of Russia's oil-producing capacity. Then came £45 million of surface-to-air missiles, a new package of Lightweight Multirole Missiles, and an instruction to ministers to draw up further sanctions, military aid and fiscal packages."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "F26",
                  "F30"
                ],
                "text": "The record suggests why. As of 13 October, two days after the ceasefire announcement, Russia was still bombarding Kyiv and Ukraine's energy infrastructure, and the Prime Minister and Zelenskyy discussed speeding up defensive support before winter."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "N1"
                ],
                "text": "My judgement is that London is hedging: it is not treating the US–Russia deal as a reason to ease pressure on Moscow."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S4:F9",
                  "F11",
                  "S5:F17"
                ],
                "text": "On the European side, the hybrid-threats partnership with Chancellor Merz and the openness to Germany joining the Global Combat Air Programme come before the UK–EU summit in Brussels on 20 November, the next date to watch."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S8:F31",
                  "S8:F34",
                  "S9:F35",
                  "S11:F37",
                  "N2"
                ],
                "text": "At home, the rail news is more coherent than it looked day by day. Derby gets Great British Railways' headquarters, with up to 450 staff, the first of up to 600 rail apprentices, and the building of a £1 billion new fleet for the north at Alstom's site. That makes the city the centre of the public-ownership project."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S13:F51",
                  "S14:F56",
                  "F57",
                  "N3"
                ],
                "text": "In Scotland, the Prime Minister's offer to work with Holyrood through No10 North and the Scotland Office already has something to show for it: the Scottish government agreed to match the UK's £10 million for two defence technical colleges. He also pointed to a choice he says he made to have Greater Manchester's buses built in Falkirk."
              }
            ],
            "stories": [
              {
                "developments": [
                  "S8:F31",
                  "S8:F34",
                  "S9:F35",
                  "S11:F37"
                ],
                "title": "Derby as the hub of the publicly owned railway"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
